toolMetadata.description:
PixelLab AI-powered pixel art generation service.

Use of this service is subject to PixelLab's Terms of Service:
https://pixellab.ai/termsofservice

This MCP server provides programmatic access to generate:
- Characters with multiple directional views
- Character animations
- Isometric tiles
- Top-down tilesets for game maps
- Sidescroller tilesets for platformer games

All generation operations are non-blocking and return job IDs for status tracking.
Requires PixelLab subscription (with credit fallback support).

**Pixel tools (free, exact, you are the artist)**: one command line, pixelart_workbench(argv=[...]), inspects, edits, draws and animates pixel art by your own decisions; combine it with the AI tools above. Start with pixelart_workbench(argv=['describe', 'start'], mode='low'|'high') — low acts directly with few tokens, high plans out loud and reviews every frame. Animated effects (spells, hits, explosions, auras): vfx_workbench(argv=['describe', 'start']) — same ids, exports to the user's My effects.

**Git access**: Clone and push game project source code with standard git.
Use `list_projects` to see your projects and their git clone URLs.

Queue a 1-direction object (10-25 generations, ~30-90s). Returns object_id immediately.

When size ≤170, produces multiple candidates (4/16/64) in 'review' status.
Call get_object to view candidates as inline images, then:
- select_object_frames(object_id, indices=[...]) to keep specific ones
- dismiss_review(object_id) to discard all
Single candidates are kept automatically.

For a fixed palette (Game Boy, PICO-8, any palette from Lospec) pass `palette`
as its hex colours: objects are drawn for them and every pixel is one of them.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__pixellab__create_1_direction_object(args: {
  // Object description (e.g., 'wooden barrel', 'forest mushroom')
  description: string;
  // Per-object descriptions when `size` produces multiple objects. Length must not exceed the object count derived from `size`. Supplying FEWER is allowed and does NOT shrink the grid: the remaining slots are generated from `description`, so they come back as extra objects in the same style, not as variations of the items you listed. One description at size=48 therefore returns 16 objects, only the first of which is the one you named.
  item_descriptions?: Array<string> | null;
  // At most this many colours (2-64). With a palette, they are picked from it.
  max_colors?: number | null;
  // Object name for reference (e.g., 'wooden barrel', 'Forest Mushroom'). Defaults to the description when omitted.
  name?: string | null;
  // Target palette, 2 to 256 hex colours as a JSON array, e.g. ["#081820", "#346856", "#88c070", "#e0f8d0"] for Game Boy or ["#000000", "#ffffff"] for 1-bit. Every pixel the tool draws is one of them; pixels you supplied (a direction you drew) stay as they are.
  palette?: Array<string> | null;
  // Optional name of the palette (e.g. 'Game Boy', 'PICO-8'); naming it helps the look.
  palette_name?: string | null;
  // Square image size in pixels (min 16, max 256). Defaults to 64 when omitted. Cannot be set together with style_images — when style_images are provided, the largest style image determines the output size. The effective size also determines how many candidate objects are produced in one shot (≤42→64, ≤85→16, ≤170→4, else 1). Multi-object results enter 'review' status — call get_object to inspect, then select_object_frames or dismiss_review to finalize.
  size?: number | null;
  // Style reference images, PNG/JPEG base64 (max 256x256 px each). Each image: {"base64": "<png-data>", "format": "png"}. The maximum count depends on `size`: ≤85 → 8, ≤170 → 4, else → 1. When empty, a default style is used based on `view`. Note: this is NOT the same as reference_image on tileset tools (which is a small 16x16/32x32 texture).
  style_images?: Array<{
  // Base64 encoded image data
  base64: string;
  // Image format
  format?: string;
  // Image data type
  type?: "base64";
}>;
  // View. NOTE: 1-direction uses top-down/sidescroller; create_8_direction_object and create_map_object use a different set (low top-down / high top-down / side).
  view?: "top-down" | "sidescroller";
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

