Generate a sidescroller tileset for 2D platformer games with side-view perspective.

This tool starts an asynchronous tileset generation that returns immediately with a tileset ID.
Use get_sidescroller_tileset to check status and retrieve the completed tileset.

Cost: 2 or 3 generations — NOT 1. (Measured over 1,186 prod charges: mean
2.6; none cost 1.) Failed jobs are not charged.

Creates tilesets optimized for 2D platformer games:
- Side-view perspective (not top-down)
- Transparent background (no background terrain)
- Platform/ground tiles with flat surfaces (no slopes)
- Optional decorative transition layer on top of base terrain

Use case: Building 2D platformer games with seamless terrain tiles.

## Creating Connected Tilesets

Use base tile IDs to create multiple tilesets that seamlessly connect:

1. Create first tileset (e.g., stone platform) and wait for completion
2. Use get_sidescroller_tileset to retrieve the base tile ID
3. Use that base tile ID when creating the next tileset for visual consistency

Example workflow:
- Tileset 1: stone brick platform → get base tile ID
- Tileset 2: wood platform (using stone base tile ID for edge matching)
- Tileset 3: metal grating platform (using wood base tile ID)

This ensures visual consistency across platform transitions.

Generation time: ~100 seconds (async processing)

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__pixellab__create_sidescroller_tileset(args: {
  // ID of existing tile to use as reference. Use to create connected tilesets
  base_tile_id?: string | null;
  // Detail level
  detail?: "low detail" | "medium detail" | "highly detailed";
  // Platform/center material (e.g., 'stone brick', 'wooden planks', 'metal grating')
  lower_description: string;
  // Outline style
  outline?: "single color outline" | "selective outline" | "lineless";
  // Seed for reproducible generation
  seed?: number | null;
  // Shading style
  shading?: "flat shading" | "basic shading" | "medium shading" | "detailed shading" | "highly detailed shading";
  // Prompt adherence strength
  text_guidance_scale?: number;
  // Tile dimensions as {"width": N, "height": N}, OR a bare int N for a square tile (same shape create_tiles_pro accepts). 16 or 32 pixels.
  tile_size?: { height: number; width: number; };
  // Pattern consistency
  tile_strength?: number;
  // Structure strictness and adherence to reference image
  tileset_adherence?: number;
  // Structure flexibility, higher values means more flexibility
  tileset_adherence_freedom?: number;
  // Platform surface/top layer (e.g., 'grass', 'snow cover', 'moss')
  transition_description: string;
  // Size of terrain transition. Controls how much of the surface layer appears (0.0 = no surface layer, 0.25 = light surface layer, 0.5 = heavy surface layer)
  transition_size?: number;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

PixelLab AI-powered pixel art generation service.

Use of this service is subject to PixelLab's Terms of Service:
https://pixellab.ai/termsofservice

This MCP server provides programmatic access to generate:
- Characters with multiple directional views
- Character animations
- Isometric tiles
- Top-down tilesets for game maps
- Sidescroller tilesets for platformer games

All generation operations are non-blocking and return job IDs for status tracking.
Requires PixelLab subscription (with credit fallback support).

**Pixel tools (free, exact, you are the artist)**: one command line, pixelart_workbench(argv=[...]), inspects, edits, draws and animates pixel art by your own decisions; combine it with the AI tools above. Start with pixelart_workbench(argv=['describe', 'start'], mode='low'|'high') — low acts directly with few tokens, high plans out loud and reviews every frame. Animated effects (spells, hits, explosions, auras): vfx_workbench(argv=['describe', 'start']) — same ids, exports to the user's My effects.

**Git access**: Clone and push game project source code with standard git.
Use `list_projects` to see your projects and their git clone URLs.

Queue a pixel-art UI panel (10-25 generations, ~30-90s). Returns ui_asset_id immediately.
Omit `pieces` for a default rounded-rect panel. Use get_ui_asset to check status.
For a fixed palette (Game Boy, PICO-8, any palette from Lospec) pass `palette` as
its hex colours: the panel is drawn for them and every pixel is one of them.

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__pixellab__create_ui_asset(args: {
  // Optional palette hint (e.g. 'brown and gold')
  color_palette?: string | null;
  // Style of the UI panel (e.g. 'wooden RPG panel with gold trim')
  description: string;
  // Named UI element types to scaffold the panel from (auto-positioned, no coords needed). Available: button, icon_button, toolbar, tab, panel, window, health_bar, avatar, triangle, pentagon, hexagon, octagon. Combine with `pieces` for custom shapes; omit both for a default panel.
  elements?: Array<string> | null;
  // Output height in px (192–688). Max is aspect-gated (see width): the two axis maxes cannot be combined — 688 only with a 16:9/9:16 partner (688×384 or 384×688), 512 only as a square (512×512).
  height?: number;
  // At most this many colours (2-64). With a palette, they are picked from it.
  max_colors?: number | null;
  // Friendly name for the saved asset
  name?: string | null;
  // Remove background after generation
  no_background?: boolean;
  // Target palette, 2 to 256 hex colours as a JSON array, e.g. ["#081820", "#346856", "#88c070", "#e0f8d0"] for Game Boy or ["#000000", "#ffffff"] for 1-bit. Every pixel the tool draws is one of them; pixels you supplied (a direction you drew) stay as they are.
  palette?: Array<string> | null;
  // Optional name of the palette (e.g. 'Game Boy', 'PICO-8'); naming it helps the look.
  palette_name?: string | null;
  // Advanced shape template (validated). Each piece needs a unique `id`, a `kind`, and an optional `label`. Allowed kinds: rounded_rect {x,y,w,h,radius}, circle {x,y,r}, polygon {x,y,r,sides,phase}. Coords are on a virtual editor canvas — longer side 0–512, shorter side scaled to the output aspect (a 16:9 panel uses a 512×288 coordinate grid, not the output size). Omit for a default full-canvas panel.
  pieces?: Array<{ h: number; id: string; kind: "rounded_rect"; label?: string; radius?: number; w: number; x: number; y: number; } | { id: string; kind: "circle"; label?: string; r: number; x: number; y: number; } | { id: string; kind: "polygon"; label?: string; phase?: number; r: number; sides: number; x: number; y: number; }> | null;
  // Optional PixelLab project ID to assign the finished UI asset to
  project_id?: string | null;
  // Seed for reproducible output
  seed?: number | null;
  // Optional style reference image (base64 PNG/JPEG, ideally <=1024px per side). The panel matches its palette, pixel scale, outlines and shading — the strongest way to make UI fit an existing game. Only the art style is copied, never its content or layout.
  style_image_base64?: string | null;
  // Output width in px (192–688). Max is aspect-gated, NOT freely combinable: square ≤512×512, 16:9 ≤688×384, 9:16 ≤384×688, 4:3 ≤600×448, 3:4 ≤448×600. E.g. 688×512 is rejected (resolves to 4:3, max 600×448) — use 688×384 or 512×512.
  width?: number;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```
